well let's speak now to the leader of  the house of commons andrea ledson ms  ledson  a very good morning to you um a busy day  ahead for you  uh one suspect can we just have  confirmed um  the way in which that full legal advice  uh will be released  well we will publish it this morning the  plan is at 11  30. why was the government  so reticent to publish in fool at the  advice that members of cabinet like  yourself had seen  on a point of principle so there are  conflicting  constitutional issues here of course  uh the house of commons has the right  through the humble address to require  papers  and having passed its vote yesterday we  will comply with that  but there's a really important  constitutional principle whereby  law officers give confidential frank  advice to government ministers and its  very existence  is not normally published let alone the  advice itself  and the reason that's so important is  because now  following the vote yesterday as i say we  will comply with it  the problem in future will be that law  officers will think twice  about the type of advice that they give  to government and government will have  to think very carefully about what sort  of advice they ask for  for fear that the house of commons might  again in future  require it to be fully disclosed so  there are two very important principles  here  one which is the preeminence of the  house of commons and the other which is  the importance of confidential and  impartial advice  and those two things were in clash  yesterday and actually i do think that  parliamentarians who  want to be in government in the future  will live to regret the decision they  they took last night well indeed but on  reflection was this strategically the  best day to have that particular battle  could you not have let it slide i mean  we have not had a government held in  contempt in this way  ever before now that might be framed in  arcane parliamentary language  but on a day full of embarrassments for  the prime minister that was a pretty big  one  well again you know this this was not of  the government's timing  um the attorney general came to the  house on monday  for a two and a half hour statement  where he answered  every single question from all members  and also published a 48-page document  setting out all of the legal  implications of the withdrawal agreement  and the political declaration  so the house of commons was in  absolutely no doubt whatsoever  about the attorney general's legal  advice so the issue yesterday the  decision of opposition parties  to hold to to to hold this vote on  contempt  was really one that they were determined  to see the confidential advice and i  argued very hard that this was a very  difficult breach  that the house had seen all of the legal  advice and that this should be a step  that they shouldn't take but of course  with the um state that that the house of  commons is in with that  it really went on to party political  lines i'm afraid and i'm i  deeply regret that i i wonder if we  might just move on to to the amendment  put forward and agreed by the house put  forward by uh dominic grave of course  your former colleague and cabinet  what's your understanding of the effect  that that has  we heard from dominic rabb a few minutes  ago suggesting look it's not binding it  can be ignored  in fact let's just have a listen to what  mr rabb said a few minutes ago  what is clear is that um the vote  yesterday  will not affect the the ultimate outcome  because votes in parliament on motions  are not legally binding so if no no  absolutely absolutely  yeah but politically i mean understand  the legal the legal argument but  politically are you saying  that that could be ignored by the  government look to to reverse the course  of brexit will require  legislation or lack of will  in number 10 downing street and i  believe this prime minister  is true to her word that she's going to  deliver brexit she always said no deal  was better than a bad deal and i think  if parliament turns this deal down we  shouldn't flounce off we should go back  and make a reasonable best  final offer to brussels but ultimately  we do need to be willing to walk away  and that's the only way in any  negotiation  you get sensible terms you're arguing  you're arguing that you're ignoring the  will of the house of commons that didn't  work out very well for you yesterday did  it  well what i'm doing is setting the clear  constitutional position which is  motions of parliament a politically uh  have weight for sure but they don't  legally  bind the government and what matters  right now both domestically  but also in brussels is political  resolve and will  so let's just be clear dominic grieve's  amendment doesn't matter  it didn't matter i said it didn't have  legally binding force and therefore  it cannot alone stop brexit or reverse  the outcome and the outcome is that  unless something changes  matters we will leave the eu at the end  of march with a deal or without a deal  i would like us to get a deal but we're  going to have to be much tougher with  brussels much  firmer and more resolute if we're going  to get them to take us more seriously  misled some you managed to hear in full  there that the the views of dominic rams  a very straightforward question will the  government honor that amendment  well dominic grieve's amendment which i  fully understand gives the house  a further opportunity to give its views  on an amendable vote basis  in the event that this deal doesn't go  through  and that's perfectly fine i understand  why he wants to do that  but dominic raab's assessment is correct  that in order for the  um for the march 29th  um leaving the eu to change  there needs to be legislation so if it's  not the prime minister's deal  through the withdrawal agreement or  another deal then the default position  will be  no deal next march the 29th  but do you not want to have i mean one  of the common complaints that has been  made by both sides of the house by  people on both sides of the house is  that they haven't had sufficient input  that there hasn't been consensus  gathered  surely mr grieve's amendment at least  suggests  if it was honored by the government it  would suggest that you are open  to new ideas open to fresh approaches  because as things stand the deal that  theresa may says is the only one on the  table is not going to pass the house on  tuesday  well you know i i really hope that  colleagues will listen very carefully  over the next few days of debate there  have been an awful lot of  misunderstandings about what's in the  withdrawal agreement and in the  political declaration  and the truth of it is that there isn't  a majority for a particular solution in  parliament  and the prime minister has been clear  she has negotiated  as hard as she believes is feasible is  realistic  with the european union it's been a very  tough negotiation  i know for many of my brexit colleagues  and of course for me too  the issue of the backstop is a very  concerning one  and that is a matter of political risk  will we end up in it  and i think what what should be clear  that misled some you you have concerns  about the backstop but as things stand  you agree with the deal  yes i i'm not happy about the backstop  but what i was going to say is  it is a matter of political risk the eu  also  very much do not want the backstop what  it does is it gives the uk  access to eu markets without free  movement  without making any contributions to  their budget without the common  fisheries policy and without the common  agricultural policy  so eu 27  okay i'm sorry to interrupt again i'm  being so so horribly on gallant but  it does rather sound to me that you  would prefer if the prime minister lost  on tuesday went to brussels and  renegotiated the deal  i'm i'm not saying that at all what i'm  asking is for colleagues to listen very  carefully  to the proposals over 